}
}

\subtree{
\title{10.4 Completeness (official accept, the candidate does not)}

\ul{
\li{\strong{S3-F7} (section 3): \code{\verb~|code~} declines a unit whose compiled body builds a closure over a variable bound to a small numeral; the reference engine decides. No wrong verdict; a decline on a shape real code has.}
\li{\code{\verb~|s3-mdata-eager-hidden-ok~} (adversarial): officially an \code{\verb~|mdata~} node hides \code{\verb~|eagerReduce~}, the comparison is plain and true. \code{\verb~|default,eager~} rejects (it sees through \code{\verb~|mdata~}, the eager comparison is false) and the reference engine rejects too: the pipeline exits 1 on an export the official checker accepts. The CANDIDATE accepts it (with the shortcuts the eager comparison succeeds). Transparency of \code{\verb~|mdata~} is the owner's decision; the row belongs to the documented divergences.}
\li{Three wrong rejects of the machine that the pipeline repairs (\code{\verb~|s3-wc-canon-fvar-full-first-ok~}, \code{\verb~|s3-wc-proj-two-decls-ok~}, \code{\verb~|s3-wc-warm-samehead-two-decls-ok~}: S3-F1 / F3).}
\li{The random programs: 200 exports (about 2,500 true claims, 100 false ones) - no rejection of a true claim, no accept of a false one, under every set and the candidate; 4 declines (S3-F7).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{10.5 Verdict on the candidate}

\p{On everything I could put in front of it - 987 hostile rows with official verdicts (mine, package S's, E1's, S2's), of which every \code{\verb~|strict~} reject row is rejected and every official accept is accepted by the pipeline - the candidate \strong{accepts no statement that is not derivable}, and I found no path to one by reading: the three additions are each sound under a condition that the code establishes (\code{\verb~|eager~}: every deferred comparison is run in its own scope before ACCEPT; \code{\verb~|skipargs~} / \code{\verb~|congruence~}: both sides are well-typed, which inference - not weakened by any switch of the default set as far as rows and reading show - guarantees). What it does accept beyond the official checker is kind T, and more of it than the default set alone: the whole \code{\verb~|X = Y p~} eager family, the congruence rows of S-F1 / S-F2, K comparisons that succeed by congruence. That is the documented price of the practical policy, not a blocker. Conditions I would attach: (1) the named switches for the two shortcuts must switch \code{\verb~|facts~} and \code{\verb~|probes~} behaviour on with them (today the options are inert without those switches); (2) the rows of \code{\verb~|work/s3/manifest.tsv~} enter the suite with their kinds, the seeded-fault rows in particular (three of the four candidate faults were invisible to my first set of rows); (3) S3-F7 is fixed or counted (it turns accepted units into reference-engine work); (4) the pipeline's strict re-check stays what decides a machine REJECT - a v0 accept there is a kind T accept at worst only as long as v0 has no kind F, which is the assumption every row above also tests.}
}

\subtree{
\title{10.6 Not established for the candidate}

\ul{
\li{No seeded fault for B1 (the same-head probe) and B2 (proofs by type); S2's own seeded faults stand for the masks.}
\li{The typed bindings (E1, 21:26Z) were exercised by the rows, not attacked on their own.}
\li{Parallel workers, the native image, the corpora and the mutants under the candidate: not mine; the gates.}
\li{\code{\verb~|eager~}: a deferred comparison inside a speculative segment that is cut, and the session rules (\code{\verb~|erSens~}, restart, forget) were read, not attacked beyond E1's rows.}
\li{The 40 kind T accepts of the earlier suites are classified by family (statement shape), not certified one by one; S2 certified 15 of its own, E1 and package S argue theirs.}
}
}
}
